== Chapter 4

#align(center)[THE KNOWLEDGE OF GOD STIFLED OR CORRUPTED, IGNORANTLY OR MALICIOUSLY.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections (Beveridge’s summary).]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The knowledge of God suppressed by ignorance, many falling away into superstition. Such persons are nevertheless without excuse, because their error goes with pride and stubbornness.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. Stubbornness the companion of impiety.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. No pretext can justify superstition. This is proved, first, from reason; and, second, from Scripture.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. The wicked never willingly come into the presence of God. Hence their hypocrisy. Hence, too, their sense of Deity leads to no good result.]

1\. #smallcaps[But] though experience shows that a seed of religion is divinely sown in all, scarcely one in a hundred is found who cherishes it in his heart, and not one in whom it ripens—so far is it from bearing fruit in its season. Moreover, while some lose themselves in their superstitions, and others deliberately and wickedly revolt from God, the result is that all are so degenerate in regard to the true knowledge of him that genuine piety can be found in no part of the world. In saying that some fall into superstition through error, I do not mean that their simplicity frees them from guilt; for the blindness they suffer from is almost always bound up with vain pride and stubbornness. Emptiness mixed with pride shows itself in this: when wretched men do seek after God, instead of rising above themselves as they ought, they measure him by their own carnal dullness, and, neglecting solid inquiry, fly off to indulge their curiosity in empty speculations. So they do not conceive of him as he presents himself, but imagine him to be whatever their own rashness has made up. With this abyss open before them, they cannot take a single step without rushing headlong to destruction. With such an idea of God, nothing they may try to offer him in worship or obedience can be counted as given to him, because it is not he whom they worship, but, in his place, the dream and figment of their own heart. Paul describes this perversity admirably when he says that, “seeking to be wise, they became fools” (Rom 1:22). He had said before that “they became futile in their thinking”; but so that no one should think them blameless, he then adds that they were justly blinded, because, not content with sober restraint, but claiming for themselves more than they have any right to, they court darkness of their own accord—indeed, make fools of themselves by empty and perverse pride. That is why their folly—the fruit not only of vain curiosity, but of unbridled desire and overweening confidence in the pursuit of forbidden knowledge—cannot be excused.

2\. David’s words (Ps 14:1, 53:1), “The fool says in his heart, ‘There is no God,’” apply first of all to those who, as will shortly appear more fully, stifle the light of nature and deliberately stupefy themselves. We see many who, once they have become hardened in a bold course of sin, madly drive away every remembrance of God, though their natural sense prompts it in them from within of its own accord. To show how detestable this madness is, the psalmist presents them as flatly denying that there is a God, because, although they do not deny his essence, they rob him of his justice and providence and picture him sitting idle in heaven. Since nothing is less in keeping with the nature of God than to cast off the government of the world and leave it to chance, and to close his eyes to the crimes of men so that they may run riot in evil ways unpunished, it follows that every man who indulges himself in complacency, having put out all fear of divine judgment, in effect denies that there is a God. As a just punishment of the wicked, once they have closed their own eyes, God makes their hearts dull and heavy, so that seeing, they do not see. David, indeed, is the best interpreter of his own meaning when he says elsewhere that the wicked man has “no fear of God before his eyes” (Ps 36:1); and again, that they proudly applaud themselves in their misdeeds because they persuade themselves that God does not see (Ps 36:2; 10:11). So although they are forced to acknowledge that there is some God, they still rob him of his glory by denying his power. For as God, Paul declares, “cannot deny himself” (2 Tim 2:13), because he always remains like himself, so those who invent for themselves a dead and mute idol are truly said to deny God. It should also be noted that though they struggle against their own convictions, and would gladly banish God not only from their minds but from heaven as well, their stupor never becomes so complete that they are safe from being dragged now and then before the divine judgment seat. Still, since no fear holds them back from rushing violently against God, it cannot be denied that, as long as that blind impulse sweeps them on, the state of mind that rules in them toward him is a brutish forgetfulness.

3\. In this way the empty excuse that many use to cloak their superstition is overthrown. They think it enough to have some kind of zeal for religion, however perverse it may be, not noticing that true religion must be conformed to the will of God as its unerring standard, and that God himself always remains like himself, and is no specter or phantom to be transformed at each person’s whim. It is easy to see how superstition, with its false disguises, mocks God while it tries to please him. Usually seizing only on things he has declared he cares nothing for, it either scornfully passes over, or even openly rejects, the things he expressly commands or in which we are assured he takes pleasure. Those, therefore, who set up an invented worship only worship and adore their own delirious fantasies; indeed, they would never dare to trifle with God in this way if they had not first fashioned him after their own childish notions. Hence the apostle declares that vague and wandering opinion of Deity to be ignorance of God: “Formerly, when you#super[+] did not know God, you#super[+] served those who by nature are not gods” (Gal 4:8). And elsewhere he declares that the Ephesians were “without God” (Eph 2:12) at the time when they had strayed from the right knowledge of the one God. It makes little difference, at least in this respect, whether you believe in one God or in many gods, since in either case, by departing from the true God, you have nothing left but an accursed idol. It remains, therefore, to conclude with Lactantius,#footnote[#emph[Instit. Div.] lib 1:2, 6. (Beveridge’s note.)] “No religion is genuine that is not in accordance with truth.”

4\. To this fault they add a second—namely, that they never think of God except against their will. They never come near him until they are dragged into his presence, and even then, instead of the willing fear that flows from reverence for the divine majesty, they feel only the forced and servile fear that divine judgment wrings from them—a judgment which, since they cannot escape it, they are compelled to dread, but which, even as they dread it, they also hate. To impiety, and to it alone, the saying of Statius rightly applies: “Fear first brought gods into the world.” Those whose minds are set against the justice of God, knowing that his tribunal has been set up to punish transgression, earnestly wish that tribunal were overthrown. Driven by this feeling, they are actually at war with God, since justice is one of his essential attributes. Seeing that they are always within reach of his power, and that they can neither resist nor escape it, they fear and tremble. Accordingly, so as not to seem to despise in all things a majesty that holds them in awe, they resort to some kind of religious observance, while never ceasing to defile themselves with every kind of vice and to add crime to crime, until they have broken the holy law of the Lord in every one of its demands and made nothing of all his righteousness. At the very least, they are not so restrained by their show of fear that they do not revel and take pleasure in iniquity, choosing to indulge their carnal appetites rather than curb them with the bridle of the Holy Spirit. But since this shadow of religion (it hardly even deserves to be called a shadow) is false and empty, it is easy to see how far this confused knowledge of God differs from the piety that is instilled into the hearts of believers only, and from which alone true religion springs. And yet hypocrites would like, by twisting and winding ways, to appear near to God at the very time they are fleeing from him. For while the whole of life ought to be one unbroken course of obedience, they rebel without fear in almost all their actions and seek to appease him with a few paltry sacrifices; while they ought to serve him with integrity of heart and holiness of life, they try to win his favor with frivolous devices and worthless petty observances. Indeed, they allow themselves greater license in their degraded indulgences, because they imagine they can fulfill their duty to him by ridiculous expiations; in short, while their confidence ought to have been fixed on him, they set him aside and rest in themselves or in created things. At last they entangle themselves in such a maze of error that the darkness of malice stifles, and finally puts out, the sparks that were meant to show them the glory of God. Yet the conviction that there is some Deity still remains, like a plant that can never be completely rooted out, though so corrupt that it can produce only the worst of fruit. Indeed, we have still stronger evidence of what I am now contending—namely, that a sense of Deity is naturally engraved on the human heart—in the fact that even the reprobate are forced to acknowledge it. When things go well, they can joke about God, and talk glibly and at length to belittle his power; but if despair, from whatever cause, overtakes them, it drives them to seek him and prompts short, sudden prayers, which prove that they were not wholly ignorant of God, but had perversely suppressed feelings that ought to have come out earlier.
